{"criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8,"evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The ZYZZsat however, does not require such constant coverage as the reef bleaching events occur over days and weeks. Thus, only one satellite is required."}],"reasoning":"The introduction clearly identifies the primary mission (Great Barrier Reef monitoring via fluid lensing), its national benefit, justification for a single satellite, and users/operators (CSIRO, BOM, Australian government)."},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6,"evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"As the satellite can be launched directly into low-earth orbit, the only orbital manoeuvre occurs at the EOL segment"}],"reasoning":"Core mission elements are addressed (Vega E rideshare, 600 km circular SSO, Mt Pleasant station with STK access analysis, passive drag-sail EOL). However, it exhibits technical misconceptions (equatorial launch velocity benefit for a retrograde SSO), contains contradictions about maneuvers vs passive design, and lacks hand calculations."},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":7,"evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."}],"reasoning":"The conclusion synthesizes the findings across launch, orbit, ground segment, and reef monitoring objectives, acknowledging launcher availability risks."},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5,"evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}],"reasoning":"The report follows a standard structure with an executive summary. However, presentation is compromised by unnumbered figure captions, incomplete sentences in the EOL section, bare URLs, and the prohibited use of Wikipedia as a reference."}],"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Equatorial launch sites do not assist retrograde sun-synchronous orbits ($i \\approx 97.8^\\circ$); Earth's eastward rotational velocity imposes a $\\Delta v$ penalty rather than a fuel saving."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The text states an orbital maneuver occurs at EOL, but later confirms the satellite has no propulsion system and uses passive drag deorbit."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Incomplete sentence: 'utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes [10].'"}],"overall":"A well-conceived Australian reef-monitoring mission with good use of STK for access analysis. Improvements are needed in orbital mechanics rigor (retrograde launch physics), independent calculations, formal figure labeling, and academic referencing standards."}
